ould be:\n\n```yaml\ncommand: mv {{ src }} {{ dst }}\n```\n\nAlso, you need to make sure that the `src` and `dst` variables are defined correctly in each task. For example, you could define them like this:\n\n```yaml\n- name: Rename python3.11 EXTERNALLY-MANAGED file if it exists\n  vars:\n    src: /usr/lib/python3.11/EXTERNALLY-MANAGED\n    dst: /usr/lib/python3.11/EXTERNALLY-MANAGED.old\n  command: mv {{ src }} {{ dst }}\n  args:\n    creates: {{ dst }}\n  when: externally_managed_file_py311_20.stat.exists\n```\n\nThis way, you're explicitly defining the `src` and `dst` variables for each task.\n\nIt appears you're encountering an issue where Ansible isn't correctly interpreting the `src` and `dst` variables, possibly due to a syntax error or missing variable definitions. Let's refine your playbook to ensure that each task correctly sets the `src` and `dst` variables and uses them properly in the command.\n\nHere’s a more structured approach to your playbook:\n\n1. **Define Variables**: Ensure that you define `src` and `dst` correctly for each task.\n2. **Use Proper Variable Syntax**: Use `{{ variable_name }}` for variable interpolation.\n3. **Check File Existence**: Use the `stat` module to check if the file exists before attempting to rename it.\n\nHere’s an example playbook that follows these guidelines:\n\n```yaml\n---\n- name: Rename EXTERNALLY-MANAGED files\n  hosts: all\n  become: yes\n  tasks:\n    - name: Check if python3.11 EXTERNALLY-MANAGED file exists\n      stat:\n        path: /usr/lib/python3.11/EXTERNALLY-MANAGED\n      register: externally_managed_file_py311\n\n    - name: Rename python3.11 EXTERNALLY-MANAGED file if it exists\n      vars:\n        src: /usr/lib/python3.11/EXTERNALLY-MANAGED\n        dst: /usr/lib/python3.11/EXTERNALLY-MANAGED.old\n      command: mv {{ src }} {{ dst }}\n      args:\n        creates: {{ dst }}\n      when: externally_managed_file_py311.stat.exists\n\n    - name: Check if python3.12 EXTERNALLY-MANAGED file exists\n